{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head 7daddceae35b6d35a3335248f3dfced3541b2b0c on task/whoami-truthful-orientation-live-refresh. Every measurement below was taken with git rev-parse HEAD in the same command and re-checked afterwards; the head never moved and no run was attributed to a different commit.\n\nStructure. Checkout /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/whoami-truthful-orientation-live-refresh is clean at the named head. 5c267901abfa17edd05be2015b0bc20919a5424d has exactly two parents, aff0fa37c970086b6bf985541969c1ff63d83f51 and 0d66c08e11f94667d1ca26f495964146e4cc8d95. git merge-tree of those two parents is c221bebc42f1c56ca3e7f5b80e5d7e87fffd61ca, which is byte-identical to the tree of both 5c267901 and 7daddcea, so the merge introduced no hand edit and the final commit changes no tree. The only tree difference from 0d66c08e is the addition of notes/2026-08-09-connectors.md, which already exists on main. Both new commits carry Rests-On the live refresh request 87426b8a, and every earlier implementing commit carries a Rests-On trailer. git diff --check is clean.\n\nObject-level equivalence to the previously reviewed work. I did not take the root promotion on trust. For each of the eight files this line of work touches, I compared the blob at eae6d130 (spike/ layout, the head whose guards were verified in the prior round) with the blob at this head. Every file is identical apart from the module import path lines changing from gitseq/spike/internal/... to github.com/generalbusiness-ai/gitseq/internal/... . No guard, bound, or test was lost, weakened, or reworded in the promotion or in either merge. ui/, spike/, and notes/ subtrees are object-identical to main (0ab96ce5, 310615aa, 8954029a), so I proved the UI and note content unchanged by identity rather than by re-running the UI suite.\n\nGuard mutations, all six re-run independently. Each mutation was applied alone in a scratch worktree pinned to the head, tested, then reverted with a clean-tree check between cases. Every one is caught.\n- Projection version equality removed from currentResidentOrientation: TestWhoamiRejectsUntrustedOrUnboundedResidentAnswers/projection_version_mismatch fails.\n- Nonnegative Frontier.Depth removed: .../negative_depth fails.\n- Required You.Name, You.Kind, You.MembershipEvent removed: .../missing_name, .../missing_kind, and .../missing_member all fail.\n- acceptSnapshot recording SnapshotSourceIncrementalTail changed to SnapshotSourceSignedCheckpointTail: TestSnapshotCachesTheVerifiedHead fails with \"accepted local append reported verified_signed_checkpoint_tail\".\n- orientationTimeout 2s changed to 5s: TestWhoamiBoundsStallsAndRejectsRedirects fails.\n- orientationResponseLimit 64 KiB changed to 1 MiB: TestWhoamiRejectsUntrustedOrUnboundedResidentAnswers fails.\n\nTwo extra probes beyond the request, because the last two guards are asserted by a literal comparison and a literal pin can be true without any behaviour depending on it. I relaxed each literal assertion to match its mutated constant so the tautological check could not fire, and re-ran. With the timeout at 5s and its pin relaxed, the stalled-resident case still fails on measured elapsed time of 5.297s against the three-second bound. With the limit at 1 MiB and its pin relaxed, the oversized rogue answer is accepted and the .../oversized subtest fails. Both bounds are therefore load-bearing, not decorative.\n\nGates. On the pinned object: make vet clean, make test all fifteen packages ok, go test -race ./... all fifteen packages ok. Head and clean tree confirmed identical at the start and at the end of the gate run.\n\nNon-blocking, main has moved. Current main is now 7d262190983da2820babd6bfa7af63962a84d9ca, one docs-only commit past the aff0fa37 named in this request, so main is no longer an ancestor of the reviewed head. The divergence is only AGENTS.md, adding the delete-merged-worktrees rule and dropping the temporary-files paragraph. This is not a defect in the change and does not affect any result above, but whoever merges must integrate that commit, and the merge is not the exact tree I tested.\n\nNon-blocking, resident trust, re-weighed rather than inherited. Before this change whoami answered only from locally verified durable evidence. It now prefers a loopback resident's answer, checked against exact local genesis and head read before and after the call, plus projection version, requested fingerprint, non-empty name, kind and membership event, the role cap, a nonnegative skip count, presence of the participant role, strict JSON with unknown fields and trailing content refused, a two-second deadline, a 64 KiB ceiling, refused redirects, and a loopback-only server URL. What is still not compared with local evidence is the content of name, kind, membership event, the roles beyond participant being present, and the depth beyond being nonnegative. A process that holds 127.0.0.1:7777 can therefore still make whoami report a fabricated identity with degraded false. I re-examined this rather than adopting the earlier reviewer's call, and reach the same conclusion for two reasons that I checked rather than assumed. First, this task adds no runtime delta at all: the tree is the reviewed 0d66c08e tree plus a note already on main, so the trust boundary is exactly the one previously accepted. Second, whoami is now the most-checked resident-backed answer in the tool surface, since status, wait, and presence return the resident's reply with no verification whatsoever. Tightening whoami alone would misplace the effort. I suggest a separate request to either compare the resident's You against local durable evidence or to say plainly in docs/reference.md that a non-degraded whoami inherits resident trust; I am not making it a condition of this approval.\n\nVerified by reading, not by experiment: the docs/reference.md paragraph matches the code on every claim it makes, including the three fallback source names, the redirect refusal, the bounds, and the omission of the actor key path from the response. I also confirmed by reading internal/app/app.go and internal/workroom/fold.go that the participant role cannot be revoked and is indexed for every membership grant, and that CapRoles keeps semantic roles first, so the participant check cannot strand a legitimate actor in the degraded path.\n\nNot run: the UI test suite and the deterministic bundle build, replaced by the subtree identity proof above; and gs merge, which I did not touch. I made no edit to the branch. Scratch worktree removed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:8e0e59afc8cf304ac54a22df1c8b32d43fb7e40b","head":"7daddceae35b6d35a3335248f3dfced3541b2b0c","verdict":"approved"}}